; host/refs_structs.bl — the pinned HOST SOURCE for the five bare struct TYPES
; `lib/beam_lisp/refs.ex` declares beside `BeamLisp.Refs`:
;
;     Atom      defstruct [:cell, :watches]
;     Volatile  defstruct [:key]
;     Reduced   defstruct [:value]
;     Promise   defstruct [:pid]
;     Future    defstruct [:task]
;
; ONE source, FIVE exact module atoms — the `priv/host/reader_exception.bl`
; idiom (one file, two exception atoms), and the reason the seed's
; `host-sources` table maps entries to modules rather than sources to modules.
;
; ── WHY ONE SOURCE CAN SERVE FIVE DIFFERENT SHAPES ────────────────────────
;
; A bare `defstruct` generates exactly `__struct__/0`, `__struct__/1` and
; `__info__/1` and NOTHING else. `compile_host_file` synthesizes that trio from
; the caller's `:struct_fields` option — which is where the SHAPE comes from,
; per atom: `[{:cell nil} {:watches nil}]` for `Atom`, `[{:key nil}]` for
; `Volatile`, and so on. So this file never spells a field name, and never
; needs to: the same bytes compiled at five atoms answer five correct struct
; surfaces.
;
; That also settles WHY this file has a `new/1` at all. A pinned source must
; spell at least one top-level `defn` (`compile_host_file` refuses a
; functions-free source), and `:struct_fields` may not be combined with a
; hand-written `__struct__`/`__info__` (it refuses that collision). So SOME
; function has to exist. `new/1` is it — and it is shape-agnostic, which is
; exactly what makes the sharing honest: it reads its own field list from the
; synthesized `__info__(:struct)` and folds the given values over the
; synthesized `__struct__/0`, so ONE constructor is correct for all five.
; `priv/host/delay.bl` and `priv/host/derived.bl` carry the same kind of
; constructor beside their synthesized surface (the pinned `Deferred`/`Reactive`
; build their values directly, exactly as the pinned `Refs` does).
;
; ── WHY THE SURFACE IS A SUPERSET, NOT A DIFFERENCE ───────────────────────
;
; The live five modules export `__struct__/0,1` + `__info__/1` and nothing else,
; so `new/1` is ONE extra export on each. A pinned module has no private
; surface (the emitter exports every top-level defn — the divergence
; `priv/host/vector.bl` documents), and a source with no defn cannot be pinned
; at all; the extra constructor is the smallest honest way to satisfy both.
; Everything a caller can observe about the TYPE is unchanged: the tag, the
; field order, the defaults, `__info__(:struct)` datum for datum, `Inspect`'s
; rendering, `Map.from_struct/1`, `Kernel.struct!/2`.
;
; ── THE TAG IS NEVER HARD-CODED ───────────────────────────────────────────
;
; `new/1` reaches its own module through `erlang:fun_info(F, :module)` — the
; `delay.bl`/`set.bl` idiom — so the same bytes compiled at `BeamLisp.Atom`
; build a `%BeamLisp.Atom{}` and at a probe atom build that atom's struct,
; matching the `__struct__/0` the emitter synthesized beside it. Nothing here
; reads a field through `(:field x)`: beam-lisp's keyword lookup is a COLLECTION
; read, and the runtime refuses one on a reference-typed struct — `new/1` goes
; through `maps/put` and `erlang/map_get` only.
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/host_refs_test.bl

(ns host.refs-structs)

(defn new
  "The struct's fields set from `values`, IN DECLARATION ORDER, over the
   synthesized defaults. Shape-agnostic by construction: the field names come
   from this beam's own `__info__(:struct)` and the tag from its own
   `__struct__/0`, so the one function is the right constructor at every atom
   this source is compiled into."
  [values]
  (let [mod (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module))
        fields (Enum/map (erlang/apply mod :__info__ (list :struct))
                         (fn [f] (erlang/map_get :field f)))]
    (Enum/reduce (Enum/zip fields values)
      (erlang/apply mod :__struct__ (list))
      (fn [pair m]
        (maps/put (erlang/element 1 pair) (erlang/element 2 pair) m)))))
